     xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"
     xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
     xmlns:onto="https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto#"
     xmlns:prov="http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#"
     xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
     xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
     xmlns:terms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto">
        <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/cco-gro/onto/v1.0"/>
        <dc:creator>Umair Arshad</dc:creator>
        <dc:description xml:lang="en">A unified regulatory ontology covering education funding regulations from four jurisdictions (UK, US, Canada, Australia), layered over the Core Compliance Ontology (CCO). Contains 1,295 named classes with 362 cross-jurisdiction 
SKOS alignments.</dc:description>
        <dc:title xml:lang="en">Unified Legislation Regulatory Ontology: Education Funding Regulations Across Four Jurisdictions (United Kingdom, United States, Canada, Australia)</dc:title>
        <terms:abstract>Ontology engineering is a complex, time-consuming, and error-prone process, and is particularly demanding in regulatory domains, where knowledge is expressed through obligations, permissions, prohibitions, conditions, and exceptions that require disciplined representation. Existing LLM-based extraction approaches improve scalability, but often struggle to maintain structural consistency in the generated ontology. We present a schema- and pattern-guided approach for regulatory ontology synthesis, in which LLM extraction is constrained directly within a target ontology rather than mapped to it afterward. The method incorporates human expertise through iterative prompt refinement, SHACL shape authoring, and repair-function design. It operates in two steps: structured decomposition of source text into typed components, followed by ontology generation guided by (i) a foundational schema that provides class and property whitelists, and (ii) ontology design patterns retrieved through embedding similarity to support structural alignment. A layered validation architecture combines SHACL conformance checking with SHACL-informed deterministic repair to address property-domain and type-discipline errors introduced during generation. We evaluate the approach through a multi-stage framework covering quality metrics, ontology correctness, and source-text faithfulness. The method is demonstrated on education funding regulations across four jurisdictions, showing that schema- and pattern-constrained generation supports the synthesis of structurally consistent regulatory ontologies.</terms:abstract>
        <terms:bibliographicCitation>Arshad, U., Corsar, D., Nkisi-Orji, I. (2026). Synthesising Regulatory Ontologies: A Schema and Pattern-Guided Approach</terms:bibliographicCitation>
        <terms:contributor>David Corsar</terms:contributor>
        <terms:contributor>Ikechukwu Nkisi-Orji</terms:contributor>
        <terms:issued>2026-05-23</terms:issued>
        <terms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
        <terms:rights xml:lang="en">CC-BY 4.0</terms:rights>
        <terms:source>https://github.com/RGU-Computing/Synthesising-Regulatory-Ontologies</terms:source>
        <owl:versionInfo>1.0</owl:versionInfo>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


